//? Title: Random-effects meta-analysis
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Let true effects vary between studies, estimate their variance τ² by DerSimonian–Laird, Paule–Mandel or REML, and separate the uncertainty about the average effect from the spread of effects a new study could show

The random-effects model assumes the studies estimate different true effects \(\theta_i\sim N(\mu,\tau^2)\), so that \(y_i\sim N(\mu,v_i+\tau^2)\). The target is the average effect \(\mu\), and \(\tau^2\) measures how much the true effects vary. Given an estimate \(\hat\tau^2\), the average is again an inverse-variance mean, with weights \(w_i^*=1/(v_i+\hat\tau^2)\). These weights are more even than the common-effect weights, so small studies count for more. The estimators of \(\tau^2\) differ:

- DerSimonian–Laird (DL) matches Cochran's \(Q\) to its expectation in closed form.
- Paule–Mandel (PM) chooses \(\tau^2\) so that the generalised \(Q(\tau^2)=\sum w_i^*(y_i-\hat\mu)^2\) equals its expectation \(k-1\).
- REML maximises the restricted likelihood.
- ML maximises the full likelihood, ignoring the degree of freedom spent on \(\mu\).

Two intervals answer different questions. The confidence interval \(\hat\mu\pm z\,\mathrm{SE}\) is for the average effect. The prediction interval \(\hat\mu\pm z\sqrt{\mathrm{SE}^2+\hat\tau^2}\) is where the true effect in a new study is expected to fall. The Q-profile interval for \(\tau^2\) inverts \(Q(\tau^2)\) against \(\chi^2_{k-1}\).

`random_effects_meta` returns the average effect, its Wald test and interval, \(\hat\tau^2\) with its Q-profile interval, \(I^2\) and \(H^2\) based on \(\tau^2\), the weights, and the prediction interval. The prediction interval uses either \(z\) (as `metafor`) or \(t_{k-2}\) (Higgins et al., 2009). The tests reproduce `metafor::rma` with all four estimators, `predict()` and `confint()` to about \(10^{-9}\) on three datasets: the BCG trials, the Normand (1999) standardised mean differences, and a homogeneous set where \(\hat\tau^2=0\).

The example applies the four estimators to the BCG trials. The simulation then draws \(k\) studies with \(v_i\sim U(0.01,0.2)\) and \(\theta_i\sim N(0.3,\tau^2)\) and records four quantities: the mean of \(\hat\tau^2\), how often \(\hat\tau^2=0\), the coverage of the 95% interval for \(\mu\), and the coverage of the 95% prediction interval. The last is the probability that a new study's true effect falls inside, computed exactly for each replicate. There are 2000 meta-analyses per setting.

```{cpp, id=re, file="figures/random_effects.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/distributions.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/random.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/meta_analysis.hpp>

#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <print>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

namespace dm = datamunge;
using dm::stats::TauEstimator;

int main() {
    // BCG trials: vaccinated cases, non-cases, control cases, non-cases.
    const double bcg[13][4] = {{4, 119, 11, 128},         {6, 300, 29, 274},
                               {3, 228, 11, 209},         {62, 13536, 248, 12619},
                               {33, 5036, 47, 5761},      {180, 1361, 372, 1079},
                               {8, 2537, 10, 619},        {505, 87886, 499, 87892},
                               {29, 7470, 45, 7232},      {17, 1699, 65, 1600},
                               {186, 50448, 141, 27197},  {5, 2493, 3, 2338},
                               {27, 16886, 29, 17825}};
    std::vector<double> y, v;
    for (const auto& t : bcg) {
        const double n1 = t[0] + t[1], n0 = t[2] + t[3];
        y.push_back(std::log((t[0] / n1) / (t[2] / n0)));
        v.push_back(1 / t[0] - 1 / n1 + 1 / t[2] - 1 / n0);
    }
    const std::pair<TauEstimator, const char*> est[4]{
        {TauEstimator::dersimonian_laird, "DL"},
        {TauEstimator::paule_mandel, "PM"},
        {TauEstimator::reml, "REML"},
        {TauEstimator::ml, "ML"}};
    std::println("| Estimator | tau2 [Q-profile 95%] | I2 (%) | Risk ratio [95% CI] | "
                 "95% prediction interval | Weight of TPT Madras |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const auto& [e, name] : est) {
        const auto m = dm::stats::random_effects_meta(y, v, {.estimator = e});
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} [{:.3f}, {:.3f}] | {:.1f} | {:.3f} [{:.3f}, {:.3f}] | "
                     "[{:.3f}, {:.3f}] | {:.1f}% |",
                     name, m.tau2, m.tau2_lower, m.tau2_upper, m.i2, std::exp(m.estimate),
                     std::exp(m.lower), std::exp(m.upper), std::exp(m.prediction_lower),
                     std::exp(m.prediction_upper), 100 * m.weights[7]);
    }

    dm::random::SplitMix64 rng(8);
    const double mu = 0.3;
    std::println("\n| tau2 | k | Estimator | Mean tau2-hat | tau2-hat = 0 | "
                 "CI coverage of mu | PI coverage (z) | PI coverage (t, k - 2) |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const double tau2 : {0.04, 0.16})
        for (const std::size_t k : {5, 10, 30})
            for (const auto& [e, name] : est) {
                if (e == TauEstimator::ml && k == 30) continue;
                double mt = 0, zero = 0, ci = 0, piz = 0, pit = 0;
                const int reps = 2000;
                dm::random::SplitMix64 sim(1000 * k + static_cast<int>(100 * tau2));
                for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
                    std::vector<double> yy(k), vv(k);
                    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < k; ++i) {
                        vv[i] = 0.01 + 0.19 * sim.uniform01();
                        yy[i] = mu + std::sqrt(tau2) * sim.normal() +
                                std::sqrt(vv[i]) * sim.normal();
                    }
                    const auto m = dm::stats::random_effects_meta(yy, vv, {.estimator = e});
                    const auto mt2 = dm::stats::random_effects_meta(
                        yy, vv, {.estimator = e, .t_prediction = true});
                    mt += m.tau2 / reps, zero += m.tau2 == 0;
                    ci += m.lower <= mu && mu <= m.upper;
                    const double s = std::sqrt(tau2);
                    const auto inside = [&](double lo, double hi) {
                        return dm::random::normal_cdf((hi - mu) / s) -
                               dm::random::normal_cdf((lo - mu) / s);
                    };
                    piz += inside(m.prediction_lower, m.prediction_upper) / reps;
                    pit += inside(mt2.prediction_lower, mt2.prediction_upper) / reps;
                }
                std::println("| {} | {} | {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | "
                             "{:.3f} | {:.3f} |",
                             tau2, k, name, mt, zero / reps, ci / reps, piz, pit);
            }

    // Forest plot: studies, common-effect and REML diamonds, REML prediction interval.
    const auto ce = dm::stats::common_effect_meta(y, v);
    const auto re = dm::stats::random_effects_meta(y, v);
    const char* names[13] = {"Aronson 1948",          "Ferguson & Simes 1949",
                             "Rosenthal 1960",        "Hart & Sutherland 1977",
                             "Frimodt-Moller 1973",   "Stein & Aronson 1953",
                             "Vandiviere 1973",       "TPT Madras 1980",
                             "Coetzee & Berjak 1968", "Rosenthal 1961",
                             "Comstock 1974",         "Comstock & Webster 1969",
                             "Comstock 1976"};
    std::vector<double> pos, lo, hi;
    std::vector<std::string> labels;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 13; ++i) {
        pos.push_back(16.0 - double(i));
        lo.push_back(y[i] - 1.96 * std::sqrt(v[i]));
        hi.push_back(y[i] + 1.96 * std::sqrt(v[i]));
        labels.push_back(std::format("{} ({:.1f}% / {:.1f}%)", names[i],
                                     100 * ce.weights[i], 100 * re.weights[i]));
    }
    std::vector<double> tick_pos = pos;
    tick_pos.insert(tick_pos.end(), {2.0, 1.0, 0.0});
    labels.insert(labels.end(),
                  {"Common effect", "Random effects (REML)", "Prediction interval"});
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.forest(pos, y, lo, hi, {60, 60, 60})
        .polygon({ce.lower, ce.estimate, ce.upper, ce.estimate}, {2, 2.35, 2, 1.65},
                 {120, 120, 120})
        .polygon({re.lower, re.estimate, re.upper, re.estimate}, {1, 1.35, 1, 0.65},
                 {26, 90, 147})
        .segments({re.prediction_lower}, {0.0}, {re.prediction_upper}, {0.0},
                  {176, 72, 43}, 3)
        .abline_v(0.0, {150, 150, 150})
        .y_ticks(tick_pos, labels)
        .margins(250, 30, 60, 70)
        .title("BCG trials: common-effect vs random-effects pooling")
        .x_label("Log risk ratio; weights common-effect / random-effects")
        .y_label("")
        .size(760, 560);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    plot.save_svg("build/random_effects.svg");
}
```
